<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>flowx</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>FlowX.Cli</RootNamespace>

    <Description>
      The FlowX command-line tool: renders the manifest as a diagram and extracts it
      from a built assembly.
    </Description>
    <PackageTags>flowx;cli;dotnet-tool</PackageTags>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>flowx</ToolCommandName>

    <!-- A tool people run dozens of times a day should start instantly. -->
    <PublishAot>false</PublishAot>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Reads assembly metadata WITHOUT executing it. Assembly.LoadFrom would run module
      initialisers and static constructors from a file the user pointed us at, which is
      more trust than a diagram-rendering tool needs.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Reflection.MetadataLoadContext" Version="10.0.0" />

    <!--
      `flowx replay` in inspect mode reads the journal. Pinned to the same version
      FlowX.Postgres uses, because the two read one store and a floating driver can change
      how a value round-trips between two checkouts of the same commit.

      This is a PackageReference, not a ProjectReference — a third-party driver, exactly as
      a third-party tool reading the same journal would use. ADR-0020 is the decision;
      the static ADO.NET surface used here is the trim- and AOT-safe half of the library,
      the same subset FlowX.Postgres restricts itself to, so constraint C2 is untouched.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql" Version="10.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately no project reference to FlowX. The CLI consumes the manifest as data,
    exactly as any third-party tool would — which is the strongest available evidence
    that the manifest is genuinely self-describing rather than only usable from inside
    this repository (ADR-0005). CliDependsOnNothingButTheManifest asserts it.

    ADR-0020 widened the CLI's *inputs* to include the journal and left that rule exactly
    as it was: a journal read adds no project reference. What it did add is
    EveryVerbButReplayRunsWithNoStore, because "this tool runs against an artifact with no
    database" used to be true by accident and is now true by design.
  -->

</Project>
